WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:05.890
Third, the large scale climate changes
that come from the concentrations

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:07.820
of greenhouse gases.

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:11.750
Here, for example, is how global
average surface temperatures

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:14.660
have changed over the last century.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:16.650
The temperature record
is much less smooth

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:19.450
than the CO2 concentration record.

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:23.650
You see warming till about 1940,
then little change for a few decades.

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:26.030
Then warming that starts
again in the late '70s,

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:28.740
slowing down over the last decade.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:29.950
Why?

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:33.830
Why does the temperature record looks
so bumpy when the CO2 concentration

00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:36.530
record that drives it looks so smooth?

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:39.470
There are several reasons why
the records look so different.

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:44.350
First, because many factors push
the climate one way or another.

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:47.590
A big volcano, for example,
puts sulfuric acid drops

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:51.320
in the stratosphere that can cool
the whole planet for a year or two.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:54.350
The same fossil fuels that
put carbon in the atmosphere

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:58.730
also pump out sulfur pollution
and other fine particulates.

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:02.120
These pollutants stay in the
atmosphere for only a few weeks,

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:06.730
but they can change the climate by
reflecting more sunlight back to space.

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:11.880
But unlike CO2 that accumulates slowly
and spreads evenly across the globe,

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:15.830
these pollutants have varied from
decade to decade and place to place,

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:20.040
as countries have changed the fuel
they use, perhaps from coal to gas,

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.250
and the environmental cleanup
technologies they use,

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:26.020
putting scrubbers on power plants
or catalytic converters on cars.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:30.290
Second, the climate has lots
of natural fluctuations,

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:32.930
like the day to day
variability of weather,

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:36.450
that are random internal
dynamics of the climate system.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:39.320
What physicists like
me like to call noise.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:43.190
The net result is that we
can be reasonably confident

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:48.280
that much of the warming over the past
half century is due to human actions.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:51.250
And very confident that
over the next century we

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:54.630
will see a great deal of
warming, due to human action.

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:57.810
But it's still very hard,
or outright impossible,

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:02.640
to know exactly what the climate
will do from one decade to the next.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:07.030
This brings us to our fourth
component, climate impacts.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:10.259
The many ways in which these
large-scale climate changes

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:13.800
influence human welfare
and the natural world.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:18.670
Many important crops, such as rice or
wheat, are damaged by high temperatures

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:22.080
during the germination phase
of their growing season.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:24.690
There's evidence that
temperature changes we've already

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:28.810
seen from global warming are cutting
global crop productivity, perhaps

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:32.040
by as much as 5%, compared
to what it would have

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.680
been had temperatures not increased.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:37.770
Although the overall crop
productivity has continued to rise,

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:40.630
as agricultural methods have improved.

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:43.940
This is small, as a global
average, but by 2050, the impacts

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:47.030
will likely be much worse.

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:49.870
If we do not restrain carbon
emissions and climate change

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:52.360
over the next century,
warming will accelerate,

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:55.900
the melting of the great ice
sheets in Greenland and Antarctica,

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:58.250
raising global sea levels.

00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:01.640
If we do not restrain emissions,
sea levels might rise by a meter

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:07.070
by the end of the century, and several
meters over the next few centuries.

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:09.020
Climate change is not just warming.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:13.200
It tends to make the dry places
dryer, and the wet places wetter.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.405
It increases the intensity
of the most intense storms.

00:03:16.405 --> 00:03:20.180
It will create a host of other
changes, small and large,

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:23.510
from a dramatic decrease in
the amount of Arctic sea ice,

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:26.380
to a loss of coral reefs
driven by the acidifying

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:28.810
effects of CO2 in ocean waters.

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:33.880
This, and all other predictions, are
subject to significant uncertainty.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:38.090
For example, with unrestrained
emissions, by the end of this century

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:41.690
sea level will likely rise anywhere
from half a meter to a meter.

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:45.680
But there's a good chance, perhaps
50% odds, that the rise will

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:47.660
be outside that range.

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:52.330
Many impacts, like rising sea
levels, occur far in the future.

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:55.840
Sea level rise is relatively
small this century, for example,

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:00.390
but it gets much bigger the next
century if we can't stop it.

